Define __printflike on QNX, as on Linux.

xo.h marks its printf-style functions with the BSD __printflike() macro
and defines it itself only on Linux. QNX's <sys/cdefs.h> has no
__printflike either, so every XO_PRINTFLIKE(x, y) became a bare "(x, y)"
and the header (installed, so every consumer too) did not compile. Define
it the same way, as the GCC format attribute qcc understands.

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/libxo/xo.h
+++ b/libxo/xo.h
@@ -47,7 +47,7 @@
  * others to turn it off where needed.  Not ideal, but functional.
  */
 #if !defined(NO_PRINTFLIKE)
-#if defined(__linux) && !defined(__printflike)
+#if (defined(__linux) || defined(__QNX__)) && !defined(__printflike)
 #define __printflike(_x, _y) __attribute__((__format__ (__printf__, _x, _y)))
 #endif
 #define XO_PRINTFLIKE(_x, _y) __printflike(_x, _y)
